WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.000
The Perseverance rover is currently driving a 3-mile journey to get to the Jezero River Delta.

00:07.000 --> 00:12.000
I'm Tyler Del Sesto. I'm a rover driver for NASA's Perseverance rover.

00:12.000 --> 00:17.000
We're here at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory, where the rover drivers are planning the drive.

00:17.000 --> 00:22.000
We use 3D monitors and 3D glasses to look at the terrain around the rover,

00:22.000 --> 00:27.000
and that 3D environment is created from images taken on the previous day on Mars.

00:27.000 --> 00:33.000
Over the past year, the rover drivers have taken a very active role in Perseverance's drives.

00:33.000 --> 00:37.000
Currently though, we're just trying to drive as far as we can every day,

00:37.000 --> 00:41.000
so we're relying more on Perseverance's self-driving capabilities.

00:41.000 --> 00:46.000
That means instead of the rover drivers choosing exactly where to turn and what rocks to avoid,

00:46.000 --> 00:49.000
we're letting Perseverance choose on its own.

00:49.000 --> 00:53.000
We give it specific stops along the route, but it chooses how to get there,

00:53.000 --> 00:56.000
and we trust it to avoid the hazards along that path.

00:56.000 --> 01:01.000
The rover's self-driving ability is very similar to self-driving cars on Earth,

01:01.000 --> 01:03.000
but there are different challenges.

01:03.000 --> 01:10.000
Perseverance needs to look at rocks, sand, and other types of hazards like craters and large things like cliffs,

01:10.000 --> 01:13.000
and it needs to avoid these to keep itself safe.

01:13.000 --> 01:17.000
This is one of our tools to look at how drives performed on Mars.

01:17.000 --> 01:23.000
This is the actual map the rover created on board using its left and right cameras.

01:23.000 --> 01:30.000
As the rover drives this path, it's using this map to identify which paths are safe to drive.

01:30.000 --> 01:35.000
Perseverance's self-driving software is a big improvement over previous rovers,

01:35.000 --> 01:37.000
which also had self-driving capabilities.

01:37.000 --> 01:43.000
Perseverance is able to process and analyze images while the wheels are still in motion.

01:43.000 --> 01:48.000
Previous rovers like Curiosity needed to stop, then take images,

01:48.000 --> 01:51.000
and then process those images before choosing a safe path.

01:51.000 --> 01:54.000
Perseverance is able to do all of this while driving.

01:54.000 --> 01:57.000
We call this a thinking while driving capability.

01:57.000 --> 02:02.000
The Perseverance rover has already broken all of the driving records from previous rovers.

02:02.000 --> 02:08.000
This means we're able to drive farther every day than the previous rovers ever were able to.

02:08.000 --> 02:13.000
The Perseverance rover has a top speed of 0.1 miles per hour.

02:13.000 --> 02:15.000
Now, that doesn't sound very fast,

02:15.000 --> 02:21.000
but that does enable us to drive over 300 yards every day and keep the rover safe.

02:21.000 --> 02:25.000
Perseverance's self-driving ability is very important to this mission.

02:25.000 --> 02:29.000
It allows us to get to the Jezero River Delta as fast as possible,

02:29.000 --> 02:36.000
and that gives the science team as much time there to study the rocks and collect samples for future return to Earth.

